{"id": "unknown-2026-07-15-004", "date": "2026-07-15", "time_tags": {"year": 2026, "quarter": "Q3", "month": 7, "week": 29, "weekday": "Wed"}, "entities": {"companies": [], "customers": [], "suppliers": []}, "topics": ["oled_penetration"], "sentiment": {"label": "neutral", "score": 0.0, "keywords": []}, "importance": {"score": 0.5, "reasons": []}, "title": "OLED display backplanes, part 1 of 7: introduction & PMOLED", "content": "<p>The display backplane is the grid of thin-film transistors (TFTs) sitting behind the pixels, switching and feeding each one. It is one of the major constraint on what an OLED display can do, and is also the component that actually consumes (or wastes, really) most of the energy, more than the OLED emitters themselves.</p><div class=\"align-center\">\n  \n  <a href=\"https://www.oled-info.com/sites/default/files/2026-06/OLED-Backplane-articles-infographic-comparing-backplanes.jpg\" target=\"_blank\">\n", "sources": [{"name": "oled_info", "url": "https://www.oled-info.com/oled-display-backplanes-part-1-7-introduction-pmoled", "type": "rss"}]}
